# The shipped defaults (E7): what a first launch with no config looks
# like. Every assertion here is a `Config{}` value made visible — the
# test fails if a default changes, which is the point: a default changes
# on purpose, in the same commit as this file.
write a.txt hello\nworld

# editor.input_style = standard: the statusline's mode chip is the
# standard handler's context label, not a vim mode.
open a.txt
expect screen contains "EDIT"
expect screen lacks "NORMAL"

# ui.line_numbers = true: the gutter numbers the rows.
expect screen contains "1 hello"
expect screen contains "2 world"

# The tree rail is visible with its default width, so the workspace file
# is listed beside the editor and the divider column is painted.
expect screen contains "a.txt"

# ui.wrap = false: a long line is clipped, not wrapped onto a second row.
write long.txt aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa\nend
open long.txt
expect screen contains "2 end"

# ui.relative_line_numbers = false: absolute numbers, so the row under
# the cursor reads "1", never "0".
expect screen lacks "0 aaaa"

# ui.stress_meter = false and no unread warnings: the statusline carries
# neither the frame-time bar nor the bell.
expect screen lacks "ms "
expect screen lacks "⚠"

# session.restore = true, editor.persistent_undo = false, ui.check_updates
# = true, editor.tab_width = 4 are asserted by the unit test
# "defaults are the shipped values" in src/config/Config.zig; the screen
# cannot show them.
